[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf("%f", ...)` decides what to consume and what bit pattern it produces.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `print_hex` and `driver` are branch-free (`len` is always `sizeof(float)`), and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` always returns 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Commands used:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - C: `cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   → `c_src/build/driver`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Rust: `cd translation && cargo build --release`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   → `translation/target/release/driver`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Tests: `cd translation && cargo test` (`tests/differential.rs`, 26 tests,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   none ignored). Each case spawns both binaries, writes the same bytes to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   stdin, and compares stdout, stderr and exit status (including the terminating
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   signal on Unix).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `SIGPIPE` disposition — exit status differed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** With stdout connected to a pipe whose read end had already been
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m closed, the two programs ended differently:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | program | result |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | killed by signal 13 (`SIGPIPE`), no output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust | exit code 0, no output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproduced by creating a pipe, closing the read end, and handing the write end
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to the child as its stdout.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library installs `SIG_IGN` for `SIGPIPE` before
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` runs. The failing `write` then returns `EPIPE`, which `print_hex`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m discards (matching C's `printf`, whose return value is also ignored), and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program falls off the end of `main` with status 0. The C program inherits the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m default disposition, so the same `write` kills it with signal 13 before it can
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m return.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `translation/src/main.rs` now restores the default disposition as the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m first thing `main` does, via a direct `signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)` call:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m #[cfg(unix)]
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fn restore_default_sigpipe() {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     extern "C" { fn signal(signum: i32, handler: usize) -> usize; }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     const SIGPIPE: i32 = 13;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     const SIG_DFL: usize = 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     unsafe { signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL); }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is inert for every other input; it only matters when a write to stdout
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fails with `EPIPE`. Covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `stdout_reader_closed_gives_the_same_signal`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Input classes enumerated, and the behaviour each one pins down
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m No other mismatch was found. The cases below all agree on stdout, stderr and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exit status; they are listed because each one is a distinct decision inside the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `%f` conversion, and several of them look surprising enough to be worth
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m recording as *confirmed* rather than assumed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | class | inputs | C behaviour |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | input failure (EOF before any conversion) | `""`, `"\n"`, `"   "`, `"\t\r\x0b\x0c"`, `/dev/null`, closed stdin | `x` stays `0.f` → `00000000` |
